{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CqFsaH2F8Jvo5PYLiu6cKp","type":"tool_result","content":"C:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-02-cargo-in-standard-crates.md-597-\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-02-cargo-in-standard-crates.md:598:[Omitted long matching line]\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-02-cargo-in-standard-crates.md-599-\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-02-cargo-in-standard-crates.md:600:That is not a stray observation; it is the spine of the book's closing argument. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 9 §1 — CNI and pod networking]* *[cross-bearing: see Ch 11 — CSI and storage drivers]* *[cross-bearing: see Ch 6 §8 — CRDs and extending the API]* *[cross-bearing: see Ch 17 §4 — the four pluggable interfaces, collected]*\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-02-cargo-in-standard-crates.md-601-\n--\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-02-cargo-in-standard-crates.md-929-\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-02-cargo-in-standard-crates.md:930:5. **The interface-and-implementations pattern itself.** CRI is the first of four published extension points: CRI, CNI, CSI, and API extensions [source: k8s-docs-extending-kubernetes-2026-08-23]. Recognizing the *move*, not just this instance of it, is what Chapter 17 will ask of you.\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-02-cargo-in-standard-crates.md-931-\n--\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-02-cargo-in-standard-crates.md-1277-\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-02-cargo-in-standard-crates.md:1278:**27 — B.** The published extension points include CRI \"to support alternative container runtimes,\" CNI (the Container Network Interface) \"to implement pod networking,\" and CSI (the Container Storage Interface) \"to add new storage types\" [source: k8s-docs-extending-kubernetes-2026-08-23]. One design instinct, applied in several places.\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-02-cargo-in-standard-crates.md-1279-- **A is wrong.** Nothing in that phrasing is a support commitment; the qualifying condition is conformance to a published interface, not historical compatibility.\n--\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-05-the-smallest-vessel.md-348-\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-05-the-smallest-vessel.md:349:[Omitted long matching line]\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-05-the-smallest-vessel.md-350-\n--\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-05-the-smallest-vessel.md-774-\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-05-the-smallest-vessel.md:775:In Kubernetes v1.22 and later, Kubernetes gets a **short-lived, automatically rotating token** using the TokenRequest API and mounts it as a **projected volume** *[cross-bearing: see Ch 11 — projected volumes]*. Long-lived ServiceAccount token Secrets, the type Chapter 4 listed, don't expire or rotate and are not recommended [source: k8s-docs-service-accounts-2026-08-23]. The type still exists; it's the legacy form [source: k8s-docs-secret-2026-08-23].\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-05-the-smallest-vessel.md-776-\n--\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-05-the-smallest-vessel.md-1377-**13. B — Legacy long-lived form; short-lived rotating tokens via TokenRequest are recommended since v1.22.**\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-05-the-smallest-vessel.md:1378:In Kubernetes v1.22 and later, Kubernetes gets a short-lived, automatically rotating token using the TokenRequest API and mounts it as a projected volume; long-lived ServiceAccount token Secrets are not recommended [source: k8s-docs-service-accounts-2026-08-23] [source: k8s-docs-secret-2026-08-23].\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-05-the-smallest-vessel.md-1379-*A* inverts current guidance. *C* is false; Secrets are namespaced like other Secret types. *D* overstates it: the type still exists and is still valid; it's discouraged, not removed.\n--\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-04-records-of-intent.md-539-\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-04-records-of-intent.md:540:[Omitted long matching line]\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-04-records-of-intent.md-541-\n--\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-04-records-of-intent.md-546- ║                                                                  ║\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-04-records-of-intent.md:547: ║   Node        PersistentVolume        StorageClass               ║\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-04-records-of-intent.md-548- ║   Namespace  (the namespace objects themselves live out here)    ║\n--\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-04-records-of-intent.md-562-\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-04-records-of-intent.md:563:> ★ **Fixed Point:** **Not everything lives in a namespace.** Nodes, PersistentVolumes, and StorageClasses are cluster-scoped, and so are namespace objects themselves. Namespace scoping applies only to namespaced objects [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23]. Any question about who may act on a resource has to start by asking which side of this boundary the resource is on.\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-04-records-of-intent.md-564-\n--\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-04-records-of-intent.md-732-A) Deployment, Service, Pod\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-04-records-of-intent.md:733:B) Node, PersistentVolume, StorageClass\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-04-records-of-intent.md-734-C) ConfigMap, Secret, ServiceAccount\n--\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-04-records-of-intent.md-761-<!-- RESEARCH GAP (2026-08-24): the hedge below is accurate but uncited — the cached configmap snapshot stops short of the \"Mounted ConfigMaps are updated automatically\" section. Re-snapshot kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/configuration/configmap/ and tag; do not delete the hedge. -->\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-04-records-of-intent.md:762:[Omitted long matching line]\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-04-records-of-intent.md-763-\n--\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-04-records-of-intent.md-1004-2. **`spec` versus `status`:** which you set, which the system supplies and updates.\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-04-records-of-intent.md:1005:3. **Namespaced versus cluster-scoped**, with Nodes, PersistentVolumes, and StorageClasses as the named cluster-scoped examples, and namespace objects themselves as the one that is easy to overlook.\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-04-records-of-intent.md-1006-4. **The four initial namespaces** and what each is for. `kube-node-lease` is the forgotten one, and its purpose (Lease objects for node heartbeats) is the detail worth pinning down. `kube-public`'s public aspect is a *convention*.\n--\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-04-records-of-intent.md-1015-| Using a namespace to separate two versions of the same software | Use labels within one namespace. Namespaces scope names; labels partition sets [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23] |\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-04-records-of-intent.md:1016:| \"Everything lives in a namespace\" | Nodes, PersistentVolumes, StorageClasses, and namespaces themselves do not [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23] |\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-04-records-of-intent.md-1017-| \"`kube-public` is enforced as publicly readable\" | The public aspect is only a convention, not a requirement [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23] |\n--\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-04-records-of-intent.md-1081-\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-04-records-of-intent.md:1082:A) PersistentVolume\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-04-records-of-intent.md-1083-B) ConfigMap\n--\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-04-records-of-intent.md-1307-| Initial namespaces | `default` (start without creating one), `kube-system` (system objects), `kube-public` (readable by all — by convention, not enforcement), `kube-node-lease` (node heartbeat Leases) |\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-04-records-of-intent.md:1308:| Cluster-scoped | Nodes, PersistentVolumes, StorageClasses, and namespace objects themselves. `kubectl api-resources --namespaced=false` settles it |\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-04-records-of-intent.md-1309-| ConfigMap | Non-confidential key-value data, ≤1 MiB, same namespace as the Pod. Four consumption paths; only the API-reading one sees updates. Immutability is irreversible |\n--\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-06-fleets-not-vessels.md-811-\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-06-fleets-not-vessels.md:812:[Omitted long matching line]\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-06-fleets-not-vessels.md-813-\n--\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-06-fleets-not-vessels.md-861-\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-06-fleets-not-vessels.md:862:You have now been told that a StatefulSet's Pods each get their own persistent storage, and you have not been told how that storage is provisioned, requested, sized, reclaimed, or shared. That is deliberate, and you should know it is deliberate rather than wonder what got skipped.\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-06-fleets-not-vessels.md-863-\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-06-fleets-not-vessels.md:864:The storage half of this — PersistentVolume, PersistentVolumeClaim, StorageClass, access modes, and the provisioning path — is a chapter of its own, and it needs vocabulary this chapter has not built.\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-06-fleets-not-vessels.md-865-\n--\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-06-fleets-not-vessels.md-867-\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-06-fleets-not-vessels.md:868:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 11 — PersistentVolumes, claims, and how a Pod's storage follows its identity]*\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-06-fleets-not-vessels.md-869-\n--\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-06-fleets-not-vessels.md-1031-\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-06-fleets-not-vessels.md:1032:Four sockets, one pattern. Collecting them into a single statement about what kind of system Kubernetes is belongs later, when you have met all four in their own contexts. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 17 — CRI, CNI, CSI and CRDs, resolved into one story]*\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-06-fleets-not-vessels.md-1033-\n--\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-08-standing-the-watch.md-567-\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-08-standing-the-watch.md:568:Chapter 4 established this and you may already feel where it goes: namespace-based scoping is applicable only for namespaced objects — Deployments, Services and so on — and not for cluster-wide objects such as StorageClass, Nodes and PersistentVolumes [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23].\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-08-standing-the-watch.md-569-\n--\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-08-standing-the-watch.md-1139-| \"Kubernetes supports the last two minor releases\" | **Three** |\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-08-standing-the-watch.md:1140:| \"Everything lives in a namespace\" | Nodes, PersistentVolumes and StorageClasses do not. This is why no quota can cap a team's Node consumption |\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-08-standing-the-watch.md-1141-| \"`cordon` takes the node out of service\" | It stops new Pods. It does not move the ones already there. That is `drain` |\n--\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-09-every-pod-has-an-address.md-386-\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-09-every-pod-has-an-address.md:387:Pod networking is implemented by a **network plugin**, and the interface it plugs into is **CNI, the Container Network Interface** — listed among Kubernetes' infrastructure extension points alongside CSI for storage, CRI for container runtimes, and device plugins for custom hardware [source: k8s-docs-extending-kubernetes-2026-08-23]. CNI plugins are **binary plugins**: Kubernetes executes them as external binaries rather than linking them in [source: k8s-docs-extending-kubernetes-2026-08-23].\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-09-every-pod-has-an-address.md-388-\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-09-every-pod-has-an-address.md:389:[Omitted long matching line]\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-09-every-pod-has-an-address.md-390-"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"325b7e30-e457-441b-91db-51b049821109","uuid":"c7577dcd-b921-49a2-a9a5-51732e1db9a6","timestamp":"2026-08-25T06:32:10.814Z","tool_use_result":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":"C:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-02-cargo-in-standard-crates.md-597-\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-02-cargo-in-standard-crates.md:598:[Omitted long matching line]\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-02-cargo-in-standard-crates.md-599-\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-02-cargo-in-standard-crates.md:600:That is not a stray observation; it is the spine of the book's closing argument. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 9 §1 — CNI and pod networking]* *[cross-bearing: see Ch 11 — CSI and storage drivers]* *[cross-bearing: see Ch 6 §8 — CRDs and extending the API]* *[cross-bearing: see Ch 17 §4 — the four pluggable interfaces, collected]*\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-02-cargo-in-standard-crates.md-601-\n--\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-02-cargo-in-standard-crates.md-929-\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-02-cargo-in-standard-crates.md:930:5. **The interface-and-implementations pattern itself.** CRI is the first of four published extension points: CRI, CNI, CSI, and API extensions [source: k8s-docs-extending-kubernetes-2026-08-23]. Recognizing the *move*, not just this instance of it, is what Chapter 17 will ask of you.\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-02-cargo-in-standard-crates.md-931-\n--\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-02-cargo-in-standard-crates.md-1277-\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-02-cargo-in-standard-crates.md:1278:**27 — B.** The published extension points include CRI \"to support alternative container runtimes,\" CNI (the Container Network Interface) \"to implement pod networking,\" and CSI (the Container Storage Interface) \"to add new storage types\" [source: k8s-docs-extending-kubernetes-2026-08-23]. One design instinct, applied in several places.\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-02-cargo-in-standard-crates.md-1279-- **A is wrong.** Nothing in that phrasing is a support commitment; the qualifying condition is conformance to a published interface, not historical compatibility.\n--\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-05-the-smallest-vessel.md-348-\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-05-the-smallest-vessel.md:349:[Omitted long matching line]\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-05-the-smallest-vessel.md-350-\n--\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-05-the-smallest-vessel.md-774-\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-05-the-smallest-vessel.md:775:In Kubernetes v1.22 and later, Kubernetes gets a **short-lived, automatically rotating token** using the TokenRequest API and mounts it as a **projected volume** *[cross-bearing: see Ch 11 — projected volumes]*. Long-lived ServiceAccount token Secrets, the type Chapter 4 listed, don't expire or rotate and are not recommended [source: k8s-docs-service-accounts-2026-08-23]. The type still exists; it's the legacy form [source: k8s-docs-secret-2026-08-23].\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-05-the-smallest-vessel.md-776-\n--\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-05-the-smallest-vessel.md-1377-**13. B — Legacy long-lived form; short-lived rotating tokens via TokenRequest are recommended since v1.22.**\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-05-the-smallest-vessel.md:1378:In Kubernetes v1.22 and later, Kubernetes gets a short-lived, automatically rotating token using the TokenRequest API and mounts it as a projected volume; long-lived ServiceAccount token Secrets are not recommended [source: k8s-docs-service-accounts-2026-08-23] [source: k8s-docs-secret-2026-08-23].\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-05-the-smallest-vessel.md-1379-*A* inverts current guidance. *C* is false; Secrets are namespaced like other Secret types. *D* overstates it: the type 