{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QUZM7kQbbmjXPwRKW33tUh","type":"tool_result","content":"1840:## The Voyage Ahead\n1841-\n1842-You have spent two chapters on the network, and you have been treating one thing as a given the entire time: that a Pod which restarts is a Pod that starts over. Chapter 5 said it directly — Pods are cattle, replaced rather than repaired — and Chapters 6 through 10 have quietly depended on it. A Service can point at an interchangeable set of backends precisely because they *are* interchangeable.\n1843-\n1844-Chapter 11 is where that assumption runs out.\n1845-\n1846-Some workloads write things down. A database has a disk, and the contents of that disk are not a detail of the Pod. They are the entire point of running it. Chapter 11 works out what happens to data when the Pod that produced it is deleted, and the answer turns out to be a ladder of three different lifetimes, only one of which survives the thing that created it.\n1847-\n1848-It also closes a loop this book left open on purpose. Chapter 6 introduced StatefulSet and told you it was about stable *identity*, not about writing to disk, and then admitted the explanation was incomplete and would stay that way until storage arrived. Storage arrives in Chapter 11, and the second half of that answer arrives with it: what a per-replica volume claim is, and why it outlives not just the Pod but the rescheduling.\n1849-\n1850-<!-- AUTHOR-REVIEW: The fact-accuracy audit flagged this next paragraph twice — the \"four\n1851-     pluggable interfaces\" claim was untagged, and the draft's count (\"you have collected two\n1852-     now\") contradicted §5's cross-bearing naming CRDs as one of the four. One of the fixes the\n1853-     audit offered was to drop CRDs from the set and repoint §5 at API extensions. That fix is\n1854-     declined here, because both binding contracts fix the set the other way: the B6 section\n1855-     skeleton assigns CRI to Ch 2 §4, CNI to Ch 9 §1, CSI to Ch 11 §5 and CRDs to Ch 6 §8 as the\n1856-     four, and the B7 canonical-forms row names \"the four pluggable interfaces\" as this book's own\n1857-     phrase for exactly that set, noting that shipped Ch 2 §4 already points at that wording. §5's\n1858-     cross-bearing therefore stands unedited and this section was corrected instead: the count is\n1859-     now three-of-four collected, which is what a reader who met CRDs in Ch 6 actually holds. The\n1860-     grouping is now owned as the book's rather than the documentation's, and the documentation's\n1861-     larger and differently-cut map is tagged. No snapshot in the corpus enumerates a canonical\n1862-     set of four; if the exam is calibrated against one, that remains a research gap. Note also"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"325b7e30-e457-441b-91db-51b049821109","uuid":"6f078ab3-ca0d-4816-bc33-f3708f32dea2","timestamp":"2026-08-25T06:33:27.065Z","tool_use_result":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":"1840:## The Voyage Ahead\n1841-\n1842-You have spent two chapters on the network, and you have been treating one thing as a given the entire time: that a Pod which restarts is a Pod that starts over. Chapter 5 said it directly — Pods are cattle, replaced rather than repaired — and Chapters 6 through 10 have quietly depended on it. A Service can point at an interchangeable set of backends precisely because they *are* interchangeable.\n1843-\n1844-Chapter 11 is where that assumption runs out.\n1845-\n1846-Some workloads write things down. A database has a disk, and the contents of that disk are not a detail of the Pod. They are the entire point of running it. Chapter 11 works out what happens to data when the Pod that produced it is deleted, and the answer turns out to be a ladder of three different lifetimes, only one of which survives the thing that created it.\n1847-\n1848-It also closes a loop this book left open on purpose. Chapter 6 introduced StatefulSet and told you it was about stable *identity*, not about writing to disk, and then admitted the explanation was incomplete and would stay that way until storage arrived. Storage arrives in Chapter 11, and the second half of that answer arrives with it: what a per-replica volume claim is, and why it outlives not just the Pod but the rescheduling.\n1849-\n1850-<!-- AUTHOR-REVIEW: The fact-accuracy audit flagged this next paragraph twice — the \"four\n1851-     pluggable interfaces\" claim was untagged, and the draft's count (\"you have collected two\n1852-     now\") contradicted §5's cross-bearing naming CRDs as one of the four. One of the fixes the\n1853-     audit offered was to drop CRDs from the set and repoint §5 at API extensions. That fix is\n1854-     declined here, because both binding contracts fix the set the other way: the B6 section\n1855-     skeleton assigns CRI to Ch 2 §4, CNI to Ch 9 §1, CSI to Ch 11 §5 and CRDs to Ch 6 §8 as the\n1856-     four, and the B7 canonical-forms row names \"the four pluggable interfaces\" as this book's own\n1857-     phrase for exactly that set, noting that shipped Ch 2 §4 already points at that wording. §5's\n1858-     cross-bearing therefore stands unedited and this section was corrected instead: the count is\n1859-     now three-of-four collected, which is what a reader who met CRDs in Ch 6 actually holds. The\n1860-     grouping is now owned as the book's rather than the documentation's, and the documentation's\n1861-     larger and differently-cut map is tagged. No snapshot in the corpus enumerates a canonical\n1862-     set of four; if the exam is calibrated against one, that remains a research gap. Note also","numLines":23,"totalLines":23}}
