{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013Esjm2eHx22vdn6RTAmEtn","type":"tool_result","content":"517:| 14 | \"Everything lives in a namespace.\" | Nodes, PersistentVolumes, StorageClasses — and namespace objects themselves — are not namespaced. | [source] |\n522:| 19 | Missing the ConfigMap size ceiling. | 1 MiB. Larger settings need a volume, database, or file service. | [source] |\n571:| 63 | \"A PVC binds to any PV that's big enough.\" | Binding is **exclusive and one-to-one**; an unmatched PVC stays unbound **indefinitely**, binding later only if a match appears. | [source] |\n572:| 64 | Reversing PV and PVC. | PV = the **supply** (a piece of cluster storage). PVC = the **demand** (a user's request). Pods reference PVCs, never PVs. | [source] |\n573:| 65 | \"ReadWriteOnce means one Pod.\" | RWO means one **node** — multiple Pods on that same node can still access it. **ReadWriteOncePod** is the one-Pod-cluster-wide mode. | [source] |\n574:| 66 | \"Deleting a PVC always keeps the data.\" | Dynamically-provisioned volumes inherit the StorageClass reclaim policy, which **defaults to Delete** — removing both the PV object and the backing storage asset. | [source] |\n575:| 67 | \"Retain means the PV is immediately reusable.\" | The PV becomes `released`, not `available`; the prior claimant's data remains and an admin must manually reclaim it. | [source] |\n577:| 69 | \"Requesting storage class `\"\"` uses the default class.\" | The empty string **disables dynamic provisioning** for that claim. | [source] |\n613:| 95 | \"Prometheus needs distributed storage / clustered backends.\" | Each server is **standalone and autonomous**, deliberately, so it works when the rest of your infrastructure is broken. | [source] |\n629:| 111 | Using the pre-2025 TAG list. | TAGs were **restructured in 2025**. Current: Developer Experience, Infrastructure, Operational Resilience, Security and Compliance, Workloads Foundation. The old list (App Delivery, Contributor Strategy, Environmental Sustainability, Network, Observability, Runtime, Security, Storage) appears throughout older study material. | [source] |"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"325b7e30-e457-441b-91db-51b049821109","uuid":"0464bd44-cc04-4c93-8eae-f1d191f40fac","timestamp":"2026-08-25T06:34:26.547Z","tool_use_result":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":"517:| 14 | \"Everything lives in a namespace.\" | Nodes, PersistentVolumes, StorageClasses — and namespace objects themselves — are not namespaced. | [source] |\n522:| 19 | Missing the ConfigMap size ceiling. | 1 MiB. Larger settings need a volume, database, or file service. | [source] |\n571:| 63 | \"A PVC binds to any PV that's big enough.\" | Binding is **exclusive and one-to-one**; an unmatched PVC stays unbound **indefinitely**, binding later only if a match appears. | [source] |\n572:| 64 | Reversing PV and PVC. | PV = the **supply** (a piece of cluster storage). PVC = the **demand** (a user's request). Pods reference PVCs, never PVs. | [source] |\n573:| 65 | \"ReadWriteOnce means one Pod.\" | RWO means one **node** — multiple Pods on that same node can still access it. **ReadWriteOncePod** is the one-Pod-cluster-wide mode. | [source] |\n574:| 66 | \"Deleting a PVC always keeps the data.\" | Dynamically-provisioned volumes inherit the StorageClass reclaim policy, which **defaults to Delete** — removing both the PV object and the backing storage asset. | [source] |\n575:| 67 | \"Retain means the PV is immediately reusable.\" | The PV becomes `released`, not `available`; the prior claimant's data remains and an admin must manually reclaim it. | [source] |\n577:| 69 | \"Requesting storage class `\"\"` uses the default class.\" | The empty string **disables dynamic provisioning** for that claim. | [source] |\n613:| 95 | \"Prometheus needs distributed storage / clustered backends.\" | Each server is **standalone and autonomous**, deliberately, so it works when the rest of your infrastructure is broken. | [source] |\n629:| 111 | Using the pre-2025 TAG list. | TAGs were **restructured in 2025**. Current: Developer Experience, Infrastructure, Operational Resilience, Security and Compliance, Workloads Foundation. The old list (App Delivery, Contributor Strategy, Environmental Sustainability, Network, Observability, Runtime, Security, Storage) appears throughout older study material. | [source] |","numLines":10,"totalLines":10}}
